For Pantheon, I’d treat a deformation as a persistent shape produced by repeated forces rather than merely a bad state.

The correction sequence is:

\text{deformation}
\rightarrow
\text{infer forces}
\rightarrow
\text{remove/redirect forces}
\rightarrow
\text{restore degrees of freedom}
\rightarrow
\text{apply corrective constraint}
\rightarrow
\text{regrow/remodel}
\rightarrow
\text{measure delta}

The crucial point is that you usually cannot fix a deformation by forcing the final shape directly. If the forces that created it remain, the system either resists correction or returns to the old geometry.

Think of a bent plant. You can straighten the stem temporarily, but if light still comes predominantly from one side, it will bend again. Better:

\text{change light field}
+
\text{support temporarily}
+
\text{allow new growth}

The same logic transfers to Pantheon.

A malformed concept, behavior, architecture, or inference has at least four components:

D=(G,F,C,H)

where:

* G = current geometry,
* F = forces presently acting on it,
* C = constraints preventing alternative configurations,
* H = historical deformation already embodied in the system.

Then correction becomes remodeling, not deletion.

Pantheon deformation repair

Suppose a concept repeatedly gets interpreted incorrectly.

Don’t merely rewrite the definition. First find the force producing the deformation:

\text{prompt bias?}
\quad
\text{ambiguous handle?}
\quad
\text{training prior?}
\quad
\text{missing context?}
\quad
\text{bad adjacency in the graph?}

Then remove or counteract that vector.

This is essentially Semantic Crabbing applied internally:

H+C=T

If the medium pushes interpretation by C, deliberately bias the corrective representation H so the realized interpretation lands on T.

Then comes something analogous to splinting: temporary constraints that teach the desired geometry.

For example, require an interpretation to pass through explicit invariants:

\text{input}
\rightarrow
\text{identify invariants}
\rightarrow
\text{generate interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{compare to invariants}

Over repeated passes, the scaffold can potentially become unnecessary because the reconstructed architecture itself better preserves the intended relationship.

There’s also a deeper biological lesson:

Old structure often has to be remodeled while new structure bears increasing load.

So Pantheon shouldn’t abruptly destroy a deformed subsystem if it still carries useful function. Instead:

\text{old path: }100\%\rightarrow70\%\rightarrow30\%\rightarrow0

while:

\text{new path: }0\%\rightarrow30\%\rightarrow70\%\rightarrow100

That is far safer than replacement.

And sometimes the deformation itself should remain partially visible. A healed structure can contain information about the force that once acted upon it.

So the generalized operator becomes:

Guided Remodeling

\boxed{
\text{detect shape}
\rightarrow
\text{infer force field}
\rightarrow
\text{release maladaptive constraints}
\rightarrow
\text{introduce corrective forces}
\rightarrow
\text{permit adaptive growth}
\rightarrow
\text{load progressively}
\rightarrow
\text{observe recurrence}
}

If it keeps deforming in the same direction, that is a particularly useful signal:

you haven’t fixed the deformation-producing environment yet.

That may be the more general rule: don’t fight the shape; alter the field that keeps generating the shape.